  "final_text": "Both objects exist, and the rule is enabled and running cleanly.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-5db1f5aa Web logs overview\" (id `cdd8a886-a87b-4a08-bacf-6457cac1177f`), default range last 7 days, with four panels:\n- **Total requests**: count on `kibana_sample_data_logs`\n- **Average response size (bytes)**: average of `bytes`\n- **Requests over time**: ES|QL line chart, bucketed by `@timestamp`\n- **Requests by response code**: ES|QL bar chart, count by `response.keyword`\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-5db1f5aa 5xx spike\" (id `1390b6db-86d1-42c4-80d0-a7af37c992de`), `.es-query` type, enabled, runs every 1m. Its ES|QL query counts `response.keyword` values matching `5xx` over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is above 10. Last execution status is `ok`.\n\nThings to know:\n- **Threshold placement:** Kibana rejects a `threshold` other than `[0]` for ES|QL rules. I put the \"> 10\" condition inside the query (`| WHERE count > 10`) and set `threshold: [0]`. The rule fires when any row comes back, so the behavior matches what you asked for.\n- **Data exploration was limited:** the ES|QL, internal search, and console proxy endpoints are disabled on this Kibana, and no Elasticsearch port is reachable. I explored the data from the data view's field list, not from value aggregations. I did not confirm the actual response codes or whether any 5xx traffic exists. The \"Requests by response code\" panel will show that.\n- **Dashboard scope:** I used the Dashboards API with a 7-day default range. The panels cover volume, payload size, trend, and status mix. I did not add per-client or per-geo breakdowns.",
